{"availability":"available","full":"jameskomo/config-drift-checker","name":"config-drift-checker","description":"CI for your coding-agent setup (CLAUDE.md, skills, hooks): pinned baselines, a canary on every Claude Code release, model and version matrix, context-cost drift, self-diagnosing reports, repair. Claude Code, Codex, Gemini.","category":"Developer tools","language":"JavaScript","stars":30,"forks":1,"created":"2026-08-27T15:53:20Z","updated":"2026-10-12T15:22:08Z","license":null,"branch":"main","homepage":"https://jameskomo.github.io/config-drift-checker/","topics":["ai-agents","claude-code","claude-plugin","evals","github-actions","llm-evaluation","regression-testing"],"demo":"https://jameskomo.github.io/config-drift-checker-demo/","color":"#25392f","ink":"#d1e5c5","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-12T16:20:08.391Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-12T16:20:08.391Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-12T16:20:08.391Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-11T07:26:18.034Z","stars":30,"forks":1},{"at":"2026-10-12T16:20:08.391Z","stars":30,"forks":1}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Your agent conventions are code. This is their CI. The rules your team taught its coding agent (CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, skills, hooks) decide how your software gets written now, and everything underneath them moves without asking: Claude Code alone ships about 25 releases a month, and the model behind an alias changes server-side with no changelog (it already has, silently, for weeks). This turns those rules into eval cases, runs them on every PR and every release against a pinned baseline, and tells you the moment something stops working: when, why, and what moved.","One suite, three agents. First-class on Claude Code (skills, hooks, release canaries, the whole drift machinery); the same cases also run through OpenAI's Codex and Google's Gemini CLIs, with live passing runs on all three, experimental labels on the newer two until full published comparisons.","Proof it works: we broke our own setup, and the repair skill fixed it. A skill's trigger description rewritten the way a careless PR would: the suite fell 1.00 → 0.56, the tripwire case read 0.00, and the report named the cause itself: the skill was discovered but never invoked, so fix the trigger wording, not the packaging. Then the repair skill restored the behaviour on its own and proved it with a green re-run, for $0.28. Both artifacts are unedited."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/jameskomo/config-drift-checker/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"442da58ec0a71d901a3699480a31ef9833162353b35521afaae5706e5957831d","extractedAt":"2026-10-11T07:26:18.083Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-12T16:20:08.391Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"Your agent conventions are code. This is their CI. The rules your team taught its coding agent (CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, skills, hooks) decide how your software gets written now, and everything underneath them moves without asking: Claude Code alone ships about 25 releases a month, and the model behind an alias changes server-side with no changelog (it already has, silently, for weeks). This turns those rules into eval cases, runs them on every PR and every release against a pinned baseline, and tells you the moment something stops working: when, why, and what moved.","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/jameskomo/config-drift-checker/blob/main/README.md"},"audience":[],"requirements":[],"fingerprint":"442da58ec0a71d901a3699480a31ef9833162353b35521afaae5706e5957831d","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/jameskomo/config-drift-checker/blob/main/README.md","checkedAt":"2026-10-12T16:20:08.391Z"},"overviewRepoUpdated":"2026-10-12T15:22:08Z","readmeSnapshot":{"version":1,"status":"saved","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/jameskomo/config-drift-checker/blob/main/README.md","checkedAt":"2026-10-12T16:20:08.391Z","hash":"442da58ec0a71d901a3699480a31ef9833162353b35521afaae5706e5957831d","originalBytes":18219,"storedBytes":18219,"truncate